<p class="article-lead">The best lessons usually start with a question: could this be a little simpler?</p><p>While working on a recent project, I stopped to look at the decisions I was repeating. It was an opportunity to slow down, understand the problem, and write down what worked.</p><h2 id="problem">Start with the problem</h2><p>Before adding another abstraction, make the current behavior clear. Write down the inputs, the expected result, and the smallest example that reproduces the issue. That simple step makes the next decision much easier.</p><blockquote>Make it work. Make it clear. Then make it better.</blockquote><h2 id="approach">A small, focused approach</h2><p>Break the work into pieces you can understand in isolation. A focused function is easier to name, easier to reuse, and easier to test when its behavior matters.</p><pre><code>// A small example of keeping intent clear.
